.TH date 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
date \- print the date and time
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B date
[\fB\-u\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B date
prints the current date and time in the traditional form, with the time
zone name.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-u
Use UTC rather than local time.
.SH NOTES
This
.B date
only reads the clock.  It cannot set it and it takes no
.BI + format
argument.
.PP
To set the time, or to move it between the kernel and the DS3231, use
.BR setdate (1).
To set it from the network, use
.BR ntpdate (1).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR setdate (1),
.BR ntpdate (1),
.BR cal (1),
.BR uptime (1)
